# Secrets and key material
# ·················
# This repository is public and data-only. The signing keys behind the fixtures
# are synthetic and their seeds live in the generator, but these patterns stay
# broad on purpose — a false positive costs one `git add -f`, a false negative is
# unrecoverable once pushed.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.crt
*.cer
*.der
*.jks
*.keystore
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
*.confidential.*
*.secrets.*
*.local.*
# Never commit non-public notes.
*.private.*
*.internal.*

# Generator: Python
# ·················
# The reference generator is the only code here. Its virtualenv and bytecode are
# environment-specific and never committed; `requirements.txt` pins what it needs,
# which is what makes the fixtures reproducible on someone else's machine.
generator/.venv/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Scratch
# ·················
# The committed contract is data (fixtures/ + schemas/) produced by generator/.
# Local experiments and scratch generator runs land here, ignored.
scratch/
tmp/
.tmp/
*.bak

# Editors and OS
# ·················
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Logs
# ·················
*.log

# NOTE — deliberately NOT ignored here.
#
# `dist/`, `build/` and `*.autogen.*` appear in the other CERTCRYPT repositories,
# where they mean "compiler output, rebuildable from source". They are omitted
# from this one on purpose.
#
# In this repository the generated artifacts ARE the deliverable: fixtures/ is
# emitted by generator/ and committed, because a conformance contract that a
# third party cannot diff and pin is not a contract. A rule matching generated
# data here would silently drop part of that contract from a commit — the fixture
# would still pass locally, since it remains on disk, and its absence would only
# surface for whoever cloned the repo expecting the full vector set.
#
# Anything genuinely regenerable and disposable belongs under scratch/.
